## 1. Replay of CS and MB in E1

| n | mean triggered | mean found | MB task (saved) | sum(t_p+1)/found | (n+1)/2 | CS task (replay / saved) | MB checks (replay / saved) | MB checks incl. rerun check |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 16 | 50.6 | 48.6 | 48.6 | 8.8 | 8.5 | 427.3 / 427.3 | 505.8 / 505.8 | 554.4 |
| 64 | 51.3 | 49.3 | 49.3 | 32.6 | 32.5 | 1607.8 / 1607.8 | 595.4 / 595.4 | 644.7 |
| 256 | 50.4 | 48.4 | 48.4 | 128.8 | 128.5 | 6235.6 / 6235.6 | 721.1 / 721.1 | 769.5 |
| 1024 | 48.9 | 46.9 | 46.9 | 523.9 | 512.5 | 24572.2 / 24572.2 | 816.7 / 816.7 | 863.6 |

All replays match the saved summary exactly; in every run MB's task queries equal triggered - 2 (the stopping rule is 'at most 2 of 100 problems unfound').

## 2. Exact posterior in the hidden-insight class (class prior)

For n in {2,3,4,6,8}, q in {2,3}, eta = 1/2 and every set S of missed probes: the predictive insight probability equals (eta/n)/(1-eta|S|/n) exactly, lies in [eta/n, 2eta/n), and the majority label is always 'no insight'. So the majority decoder's run ends at (p, n, bottom) until the insight is found, and the failure of the decoder is exactly (#triggered unfound)/m, as the code computes.

## 3. Theorem 3 against E1, and a Monte Carlo check of the bound

Theorem 3 with q -> infinity: failure >= eta (1 - 2Q/(nm)), so failure <= 0.02 needs Q >= (nm/2)(1 - 0.02/eta) = 0.48 nm task queries (on average over the prior; E1 uses 10 fixed humans, so this is only a sanity check).

| n | lower bound 0.48nm | CI task | US(eta/2n) task | CI / bound | US / bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 16 | 768 | 4,967 | 1,173 | 6.47 | 1.53 |
| 64 | 3,072 | 18,085 | 4,526 | 5.89 | 1.47 |
| 256 | 12,288 | 76,929 | 18,344 | 6.26 | 1.49 |
| 1024 | 49,152 | 316,170 | 74,441 | 6.43 | 1.51 |

CI's mean is close to the coupon-collector estimate m n (H_50 - H_2) = 3.0 m n; the low-threshold uncertainty sampler (the 'cheating' scan) is about 4x cheaper than CI at every n and within a factor 1.6 of the lower bound.

Monte Carlo (eta = 8 eps = 1/4, q = 2, 20000 draws of theta each), best-effort scanning protocol with Q task queries:

| n | m | Q | Q/(nm) | simulated failure | Theorem 3 lower bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 8 | 40 | 16 | 0.050 | 0.1168 | 0.1125 |
| 8 | 40 | 40 | 0.125 | 0.1061 | 0.0938 |
| 8 | 40 | 80 | 0.250 | 0.0911 | 0.0625 |
| 8 | 40 | 160 | 0.500 | 0.0576 | 0.0000 |
| 32 | 20 | 32 | 0.050 | 0.1171 | 0.1125 |
| 32 | 20 | 80 | 0.125 | 0.1058 | 0.0938 |
| 32 | 20 | 160 | 0.250 | 0.0867 | 0.0625 |
| 32 | 20 | 320 | 0.500 | 0.0556 | 0.0000 |

The simulated failure is at or above the bound in every case, within Monte Carlo error (standard error about 0.002). The bound is not tight for large Q: it reaches 0 at Q = nm/2, while scanning needs about Q = nm(1 - eta/2) to find every insight.

